title: expand_class() forces a full /openapi/v2 download — pure name resolution costs
  a cluster roundtrip
updated: 2026-08-18T17:48:22Z
---

expand_class is delegated (handles => [...]) to the lazy 'k8s' attribute, which builds IO::K8s->new(resource_map => $self->resource_map). resource_map defaults to resource_map_from_cluster => 1, so the first expand_class call runs _load_resource_map_from_cluster -> fetch_resource_map -> GET /openapi/v2 and decodes the full OpenAPI spec (multi-MB on a real cluster).

A caller that only wants 'Pod' -> IO::K8s::Api::Core::V1::Pod, an answer already sitting in %DEFAULT_RESOURCE_MAP, pays for the whole spec. Same for new_object, inflate, load — every delegated method drags resource_map in.

Note the irony: IO::K8s 1.107 explicitly stopped loading ~110 modules in BUILD because it 'cost ~0.4s even for a caller that only wanted a ConfigMap' (comment above %DEFAULT_RESOURCE_MAP). The cluster fetch reintroduces that cost one layer up, over the network.

It is lazy and cached per instance, so it is once per process, and _load_resource_map_from_cluster carps rather than dying. Not a correctness bug — a layering one.

Options: resolve against IO::K8s->default_resource_map first and only fall through to the cluster map on a miss; or split name resolution from the cluster-backed map so expand_class does not force the fetch.

Found while auditing p5-mcp-k8s, whose plural lookup calls expand_class per unknown Kind and then discards the result.
ANALYSIS 2026-08-16 - the question is not "default map first or cluster map first"

Re-checked against IO::K8s 1.108 (../io-k8s-p5, installed; cpanfile pins 1.107). The
premise of this ticket - "the answer is already sitting in %DEFAULT_RESOURCE_MAP" - holds
for built-in kinds only. For everything else the cluster map is not merely expensive, it
is wrong, and resolving default-first would freeze that wrongness in place instead of
removing it. What has to be decided is whether this distribution keeps deriving class
names at all.

1. The cluster map invents class names that do not exist
------------------------------------------------------------------
fetch_resource_map (lib/Kubernetes/REST.pm:272-298) derives the class from the group name
by string rule, via _io_k8s_namespace_for_group: group 'cert-manager.io' becomes
'Api::Certmanager::V1::Certificate'. That rule is only correct for the kinds IO::K8s ships
under IO::K8s::Api::. cert-manager lives at IO::K8s::CertManager::V1::Certificate, Cilium
at IO::K8s::Cilium::..., and a user CRD has no generated class at all.

Measured against IO::K8s 1.108 with a cluster-shaped map
{ Certificate => 'Api::Certmanager::V1::Certificate' }:

  expand_class('Certificate')                            -> IO::K8s::Api::Certmanager::V1::Certificate  (no such module)
  expand_class('Certificate', 'cert-manager.io/v1')      -> undef
  IO::K8s->new(with => ['IO::K8s::CertManager'])
    expand_class('Certificate', 'cert-manager.io/v1')    -> IO::K8s::CertManager::V1::Certificate       (correct)

The client resolves short names only (list/get/... call expand_class($short_class), then
_inflate_object at lib/Kubernetes/REST.pm:555-558 passes the resulting class straight to
json_to_object), so it takes the first line: a dead class name, and the failure surfaces
at load time inside IO::K8s, not at map build time.

Worse, the bad entry is sticky. add() qualifies both sides of a short-name collision and
leaves the incumbent on the bare key (lib/IO/K8s.pm:386-400), so registering the correct
provider afterwards does not repair it:

  $k8s->add('IO::K8s::CertManager')
    expand_class('Certificate')                          -> IO::K8s::Api::Certmanager::V1::Certificate  (still dead)
    expand_class('Certificate', 'cert-manager.io/v1')    -> IO::K8s::CertManager::V1::Certificate

So on the exact kinds resource_map_from_cluster exists for, the cluster map is worse than
no cluster map, and it cannot be fixed from the outside on the path this client uses.

2. IO::K8s already solves this, and we hand it none of it
------------------------------------------------------------------
IO::K8s 1.108 takes openapi_spec => $spec and auto-generates working classes for unknown
kinds (IO::K8s::AutoGen, reached from expand_class and _resolve_mapped whenever
has_openapi_spec), takes with => [...] / add() for shipped providers, takes
class_namespaces => [...] for the consumer's own classes, and carries domain-qualified
'$apiVersion/$Kind' keys in its own map.

lib/Kubernetes/REST.pm:108-113 passes exactly one of these: resource_map. has_openapi_spec
is therefore false for every client this distribution builds, and AutoGen is dead code from
here. We download the very spec that would make it work, throw the spec away, and keep a
name table derived from it by a rule that only covers the built-ins.

That is the layering defect. #15's cost is a symptom of it.

3. What is actually on the table
------------------------------------------------------------------
A. Keep deriving names, resolve default-first (the option in the ticket body).
   Cheap. Removes the fetch for built-ins. Locks in section 1: CRDs keep resolving to
   dead classes, and the flag half-stops meaning what its POD says
   ("may not match your cluster version").

B. Hand IO::K8s the spec instead of a derived map, when resource_map_from_cluster is on.
   Fixes section 1 - CRDs get real classes from AutoGen. Does NOT fix the cost: openapi_spec
   is a plain ro attribute, so it has to be there at IO::K8s construction, and the lazy k8s
   attribute still triggers the download on the first delegated call. Correctness and cost
   are separate decisions.

C. Default resource_map_from_cluster to 0, and make 1 mean B.
   Nobody pays for a spec they did not ask for, whoever asks gets cluster-accurate
   resolution that actually works for CRDs. Breaking change on a documented default,
   needs its own release and a Changes bullet. Closest to the direction IO::K8s 1.107
   already took ("do not load 110 modules for a caller who wants a ConfigMap").

D. Orthogonal, worth deciding at the same time: should new() pass class_namespaces and
   with/add through to IO::K8s? Today the POD (lib/Kubernetes/REST.pm:151) sends people to
   $api->k8s->add(...), and section 1 shows that is a dead end once the cluster map has
   claimed the short name.

Constraints on any of these: fetch_resource_map is a documented public =method, so removing
it is the tombstone protocol in Kubernetes-REST-Deprecated, not a deletion. _io_k8s_namespace_for_group
has exactly one caller (fetch_resource_map, lib/Kubernetes/REST.pm:289) plus t/27_v0_group_classes.t;
_io_k8s_namespace_for_group_path serves the v0 layer and is untouched by all of this.
Net::Async::Kubernetes could not be checked - ../p5-net-async-kubernetes is not checked out
here - but the resource map is not among its seven published seam methods.

Not urgent by any reading: the map is lazy and cached per instance, _load_resource_map_from_cluster
carps rather than dies, and p5-mcp-k8s (MCP/K8s.pm:416-425) has already guarded its call and
points back at this ticket. See also #17 for the duplicate /openapi/v2 download, which is
decision-free and can land regardless of what is chosen here.
Beziehung zu io-k8s karr #35 geprueft (2026-08-16): verwandt, aber nicht gekoppelt. Beide sind Symptome davon, dass expand_class mehr tut als reine Namensaufloesung -- dort ein require-Probe auf beliebige \@INC-Pakete, hier ein Cluster-Roundtrip ueber die resource_map. Die Fixes sind disjunkt: #35 lebt vollstaendig in lib/IO/K8s.pm, #15 vollstaendig hier im Layering des lazy k8s-Attributs. Einziger Beruehrungspunkt: #35 Teil 2 (struct_to_object re-expandiert nicht mehr) halbiert die expand_class-Aufrufe pro new_object von 2 auf 1 -- mildert #15 marginal, loest es nicht, da der Fetch lazy und pro Instanz gecached ist. #15 ist also weiterhin eigenstaendig anzugehen.
Nachtrag: die Messungen oben liefen gegen den Arbeitsstand von ../io-k8s-p5 (1.108, unveroeffentlicht). Auf dem released IO::K8s 1.107, das cpanfile pinnt, kommen exakt dieselben vier Ergebnisse heraus - tote Klasse auf dem Kurznamen, undef auf dem qualifizierten Pfad, add() repariert den Kurznamen nicht, with-Provider loest korrekt auf. Die Analyse haengt also an keinem unveroeffentlichten IO::K8s-Verhalten. openapi_spec und IO::K8s::AutoGen (Option B) sind in 1.107 bereits enthalten, ein Versionsbump waere dafuer nicht noetig.
Fixed in fe376de: expand_class is now a client method with a cheap path against the built-in IO::K8s map (class-method expand_class), falling through to the cluster-backed k8s instance only when the built-in map cannot produce a loadable class. Caller-supplied resource_map and post-fetch behavior unchanged. Regression test t/36_expand_class_no_fetch.t. Options B-D from the 2026-08-16 analysis (handing IO::K8s the spec / flag default change / class_namespaces passthrough) deliberately not taken - out of scope for the roundtrip fix.
